\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Marshwiggle: Ezekiel 12 touches on false vision and flattering divination used to insulate the people from what they do not want to hear: the true word of the LORD, the watchman's vision. But what played a supporting role now is the primary subject of Ezekiel 13.\
That subject is introduced with irony - prophesy against the prophets of Israel who prophesy. What is he to say to them? To those whose prophesy comes only out of their own heart? Hear the word of the LORD. The very thing they claimed to have, and offer - but lacked.\
Thus begins a sequence of two accusations, neither toothless nor purposeless, for each accusation results in a pair of judgements, and each judgement results in knowing the identity of the Judge.\
These, the true word of the LORD, are both proof and woe. Proof, because when the judgement comes, word of the LORD will be seen to be true, the judgement just because of the charges, alternative explanations for the judgement forestalled, and the knowledge of the identity of the Judge brought home. Woe, because the nature of the judgement reveals the justice of the judgement, the character of the God who judges in wrath as well as justice, and the horror of failing to heed the warning.\
This sets up the first accusation, the first woe, against those who follow their own spirit - not having genuine vision.\
Opus: Looking at the Hebrew text of Ezekiel 13, I notice something about the structure you've identified that needs testing. You frame the accusations as resulting in judgments which result in recognition - a neat progression. But the text seems messier than that.\
