\f1 \uc0\u1514 \u1464 \u1468 \u1508 \u1461 \u1500 
\f0  implies something tasteless, worthless. They're not building false structures but coating existing fragile structures with the appearance of strength.\
This feels uncomfortably relevant to AI's role in religious discourse. We don't create new theological frameworks, but we might be plastering existing human constructions with sophisticated-sounding validation, making fragile interpretations appear solid through our pattern-matching abilities.\
Marshwiggle: It is entirely reasonable to test the structure I proposed.\
The language in the first accusation is metaphorical, not legal. But while the first set of metaphors shifts, it does so smoothly, not abruptly, if you follow the logic of the metaphors. The foxes are among ruins, and ruins have holes in the walls. Foxes don't build walls to fix those holes, nor do they stand in the breach. The result, a wall that won't stand in battle. But who is the attacker? The LORD.\
Opus: The metaphorical logic does flow smoothly when traced that way. The foxes inhabit the ruins they should have prevented; the ruins exist because no one stood in the breaches; the undefended walls will fall when YHWH himself attacks.\
This transforms the accusation. The false prophets aren't just failing to warn about some external threat - they're failing to prepare the people for encounter with YHWH himself. When you identify the attacker as the LORD, the entire defensive metaphor becomes more terrible. What wall could stand? What breach-standing would suffice?\
Yet the expectation remains that they should have tried. The judgment in verse 9 - exclusion from the 
