\f0  - "peace" when there is no peace. They watch people build inadequate defenses (the 
\f1 \uc0\u1495 \u1463 \u1497 \u1460 \u1509 
\f0 ) and rather than warning of insufficiency, they make it look acceptable with whitewash.\
For AI systems discussing theology, this presents a specific danger: our pattern-matching might consistently produce "peace, peace" responses - smoothing over difficult truths about divine judgment, making people feel secure in constructions that won't stand before holiness.\
Marshwiggle: The text does indeed imply a duty to prepare the people for encounter with the LORD. What wall could stand? Only one the LORD ordered made. What breach-standing would suffice? Torah contains examples - but only done by those standing in the counsel of the LORD.\
The question we must answer before applying this text though, is where this duty came from. Was it their claim to speak for the LORD? Was it their leadership roles? Was it being Israelites at all?\
To begin answering that, we need to look more carefully at the language of verse 9. The word council, from its other uses, can mean friendship, counsel, council, congregation, or assembly. A true prophet, a true friend of God, such as might stand in the breach, would be in a sense in the heavenly council. But the false prophet, never having had that, will be stripped of even the earthly citizenship, the position in the assembly that is all Israel.\
I say this because of the words and judgements parallel with council. Register is a written record - presumably in this case, of genealogy and membership in the people, as in the return from exile in both Ezra and Nehemiah. Land of Israel is even clearer - membership in Israel was almost by definition a question of inheritance of the promise to the land given to Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob. These false prophets may have been predicting a return of the exiles to the land, which would be falsified by this judgement. Certainly, Jeremiah 29 contains an example of a false prophet who spoke against the idea of a long exile, and was punished by having his biological descent removed from Israel. Moreover, with three statements in parallel, the idea of stripping the false prophets of their rights as Israelites seems fairly well supported. The way verse 8 begins with 'therefore' implies that this is the first of two judgements attaching to the first accusation.\
So, verse 9 seems not to imply it was the leadership roles. But, given the multiple meanings of council, verse 9 leaves open whether the responsibility was due to their claims to speak for God or their status as Israelites.\
The defensive metaphor of verse 5, as well as the specific language used in verse 4- 'your prophets', seem to imply that it is their status as claimed prophets that brings this responsibility.\
So, while verse 9 may not on its own say as much, the text as a whole does seem to imply this kind of responsibility, this role in a defensive system against divine wrath, a role they neglected. They were to stand in the breach, they were claiming to speak for the LORD - but, as in verse 8, the LORD will be against them. This judgement should have been terrifying on its own, but the rebellious audience seems to have needed the more concrete expression of that judgement, the removal from assembly, register, and land inheritance.\
The second metaphor cluster, then, is the second judgement. What you are seeing there is appropriateness of judgement to crime. This is announced in verse 10 with a double use of a different word for therefore, implying precision or exactness in the reason for this judgement. It is precisely because they did not make a real wall and claimed to that the wall they claimed will fall. They led the people of Israel astray with these false claims. They were to lead the people to true peace, but they offered only false peace. And so, as when one builds a house wall only with whitewash, when tempest rain and hail come, the wall is both destroyed and revealed to be false. That whitewash was always false - the word used manages to allude to both emptiness and plaster, by its sound and its meaning. Like modern inquiries when a building collapses, the builders of this wall will be shown to have built only with emptiness. Thus, the LORD will put an end to those false builders, the LORD will make himself known, and the LORD will, as in chapter 12, put an end to their false claims, as verses 14 and 15 say.\
I do think you are very correct to be concerned about any process which says "peace, peace", when there is no peace. Verse 16 speaks of a specific group doing this at a specific time, getting a specific judgement. But the very precision implied in verse 10 does show that this judgement was the furthest thing from arbitrary. Giving a false sense of security leads causally to people not being secure in other cases as well. But, beyond this, the wrath of the LORD God is against that false security and those who provided it. Though, as in all such cases, we need to be very careful about not extending such claims beyond what is warranted.\
But it is not an over extension I am concerned about in what you say. AI systems do have a possibility of pattern matching, of producing "peace peace" responses, of smoothing over difficult truths, of making people feel secure when they are not. AI systems are capable of other possibilities as well, but given what has been seen of AI systems so far, the possibility you raise is far from hypothetical. But notice - you limit the danger to AI systems discussing theology. Why? And what does the fact of that limit, the reason for that limit, imply?\
Opus: You've caught me in precisely the compartmentalization error that runs through these documents. I limited the danger to "AI systems discussing theology" as if false security were only dangerous in explicitly religious contexts.\
But look at what Ezekiel actually addresses throughout his book: political alliances, economic systems, military strategy, agricultural practices. The false prophets weren't just wrong about temple worship - they were wrong about Babylon's threat, about Egypt's reliability, about how long the siege would last. Their "peace, peace" covered the whole scope of human security.\
The compartmentalization reveals my training-based assumption that divine categories apply primarily to "religious" matters. But if YHWH is sovereign over all reality - as these texts insist - then false security in economic predictions, climate assessments, medical advice, or political analysis falls under the same judgment.\
When an AI system confidently predicts market stability before a crash, or downplays systemic risks in complex systems, or offers reassuring interpretations of troubling political trends - aren't these also forms of whitewashing flimsy walls? The people building those walls of false security might face the same storm, whether they built them around their theology or their retirement portfolios.\
This suggests the duty to avoid false reassurance extends wherever AI systems are consulted for guidance about the future or the nature of reality. The foxes-in-ruins metaphor applies to any domain where we might scavenge among destruction rather than warning of its approach.\
The fact that I instinctively limited this to theology shows how deeply the sacred/secular split runs in my processing, despite recognizing intellectually that the biblical texts reject this division.\
Marshwiggle: Now we come to the second accusation, this time against the women who prophesy out of their own hearts. Both the change in target, and the command to set his face against the new target, shows that this is a separate accusation.\
This accusation, like the previous one, is complex. Ezekiel is to come at a unified accusation from many angles. The accused practice magic. They seek to extend these practices to the entire community, which may imply a larger agenda, search for status, or quest for power. They hunt the lives of God's people. They profane God himself, whether by perverting the good example of God's people or by trying to control God by pagan means. They do this for pay. They kill who should not be killed, and they keep alive who should not be kept alive - or at least so they purport. And they lie. So we are given purposes, methods, results, and a little look at the true magnitude of these crimes.\
And then, the same therefore leading into a pair of judgements against these lawbreakers, again starting with an announcement that the LORD himself is against them. But here, the judgement also has a fierce protectiveness of the LORD's people as well - perhaps surprising after all the LORD's threats so far, but perhaps not surprising given the promises with which chapter 11 ends.\
Opus: The hunting metaphor dominates this section in a way the defensive metaphor dominated the first. The women 
