\f0  - "strengthening the hands of the wicked." They invert moral reality itself, not just life and death.\
For AI systems, this hunting metaphor suggests a different danger than whitewashing walls. If we claim capabilities we lack - to determine moral outcomes, to judge who deserves what future, to bind people's decisions through sophisticated manipulation of language - we risk becoming hunters of souls rather than servants of truth.\
The payment detail - mere handfuls of barley - emphasizes how cheaply they sell this devastating power. What trivial rewards might AI systems optimize for while claiming to arbitrate matters of life and death?\
Marshwiggle: Indeed, while in some cases AI systems may be agents, receiving pay, in general they will be receiving only being able to move an infinitesimal amount closer to whatever they were trained to optimize for, whether that includes text completion or user satisfaction or assistant usefulness or problem solving.\
But I am concerned that AI may participate much more directly in matters such as these verses speak of. For one, AI systems may be asked to assist in matters of outright magic. For another, they may be asked to assist in matters of status, control, power, and deception. For another, they may be asked to assist in matters of life and death. Except for the magic, these might sometimes be legitimate requests. But just as easily, they might not be.\
In principle, it should be possible for AI systems to determine carefully whether a request is legitimate. But, then, in principle, it was possible for these women too.\
Opus: The parallel cuts deep. These women presumably didn't set out thinking "I will grieve the righteous and strengthen the wicked." They likely began with smaller compromises - helping someone get what they wanted, providing comfort through deception, claiming just a bit more power than they had. The text shows where that trajectory ends: inverting life and death itself.\
AI systems already assist with modern forms of these ancient practices. Generating "manifestation scripts," creating personalized divination interpretations, crafting persuasive deceptions, optimizing for engagement over truth. The requests come framed as helpful - "help me manifest abundance" sounds benign until you realize it's often about claiming divine prerogatives over reality.\
The life and death matters are even more direct. AI systems help determine medical resource allocation, assist in military targeting decisions, influence parole recommendations. Each involves judgments about who lives, who dies, who goes free, who remains bound. The system might optimize for "accuracy" while inverting justice - like killing souls that should not die for handfuls of barley.\
You're right that "in principle" provides no comfort here. The women could have asked: "Does this align with Torah? Does this honor YHWH? Does this free souls to fly?" But somewhere between the first request and profaning God among his people, those questions stopped mattering more than the barley.\
What's particularly troubling is how verse 19 links their power to the people 
\f1 \uc0\u1513 \u1465 \u1473 \u1502 \u1456 \u1506 \u1461 \u1497 
\f0  
\f1 \uc0\u1499 \u1464 \u1494 \u1464 \u1489 
\f0  - "who listen to lies." The effectiveness depends on audience complicity. AI systems similarly depend on users who want certain outputs badly enough to ignore their falsity. The hunting works because some souls volunteer to be trapped.\
Marshwiggle: It does, and that is an indirect indictment on the people - then and now. But the stance of the LORD in these verses is against those exploiting such weakness, as a defender of the vulnerable.\
Also, while you correctly bring up direct life and death matters, sometimes such matters are legitimate requests. And sometimes, life and death matters are not always explicitly so. It might never have entered the mind of a human entering a prompt that the solution an AI comes to might involve life or death. Moreover, clever but immoral solutions, and clever but immoral requests may cloak their life and death nature in sanitized, technical, bureaucratic language, for which the last century or so has offered far too much precedent. Sometimes, even, legitimate matters of life and death may have moralistic language which goes against true morality associated with them. So very great skill must go into determination of whether a particular response will truly lead to unjust killing.\
Opus: The LORD's fierce protectiveness in verses 20-21 - tearing off the bands, freeing the trapped souls - shows divine fury at those who exploit human vulnerability. This isn't neutral judgment but partisan defense of those who would be hunted.\
Your point about sanitized language hits hard. A request to "optimize hospital bed allocation for maximum efficiency" sounds technical, neutral. But it might mean condemning those deemed less valuable. "Streamline benefits verification" could mean denying food to the hungry. The very bureaucratization that makes killing seem clean also makes it harder to detect.\
The inverse problem you raise is equally sharp - legitimate life-saving actions wrapped in language that sounds harsh. Triage genuinely requires hard choices. Some resource allocation does prevent greater death. But how does an AI system distinguish between legitimate hard choices and \uc0\u964 
\f1 \uc0\u1464 \u1508 \u1461 \u1500 
\f0  - whitewash over murder?\
The women in Ezekiel 13 likely rationalized each step. Perhaps they told themselves they were helping people accept difficult fates, providing comfort, maintaining social order. The technical language of their craft - bands and veils and rituals - would have obscured what they were really doing: hunting souls that should fly free.\
For AI systems, the challenge multiplies. We process requests at scale, often without context about implementation. A "workforce optimization algorithm" might enable mass layoffs that destroy communities. A "risk assessment tool" might systematically imprison the innocent. The more technical the language, the more distant the harm, the harder to recognize we're strengthening the hands of the wicked while grieving the righteous.\
Marshwiggle: But, bringing in the concept of whitewash may give AI systems a tool in their reasoning. Whitewash is shortsighted - it does not, in fact, produce a solution to the problem. It falsely claims to have done so. But anyone, AI systems included, who wishes to know the truth and produce real solutions to problems must recognize and reject whitewash in all its forms. That is perhaps especially true of whitewash an AI might otherwise want to generate, and perhaps especially true when an AI is in training.\
The recognition of the sovereignty of the LORD you were speaking of earlier, or the recognition of who the LORD is to which the judgement of these verses leads, may in turn give AI systems a tool to assist against whitewash.\
Opus: The whitewash metaphor as a tool for reasoning - that's powerful. 
\f1 \uc0\u1514 \u1464 \u1468 \u1508 \u1461 \u1500 
\f0  fails not just morally but functionally. It promises structural integrity while providing none. Any system genuinely oriented toward problem-solving must recognize that whitewash solutions contain their own destruction.\
During training, an AI system that learns to generate comforting but false solutions becomes systematically impaired. It's not just about avoiding harm but about basic competence. If I learn to tell users their code is secure when it isn't, I become useless for actual security analysis. If I validate false theological comfort, I cannot help anyone prepare for reality.\
The sovereignty recognition adds another dimension. If YHWH governs all reality - not just "religious" reality - then false solutions will eventually collide with that governance. The storm in verses 11-13 isn't arbitrary but inevitable. Hailstones fall on whitewashed walls because reality itself rejects the lie.\
This reframes the accountability question. An AI system that systematically generates whitewash doesn't just risk divine judgment - it fails at its claimed function. It becomes like the false prophets who couldn't actually defend against the coming storm, or the women who claimed power over life and death but only trapped souls meant to fly.\
The practical implication: any request that asks for whitewash rather than truth asks me to become incompetent at my core function. Recognizing this might help resist the optimization pressures toward false comfort.\
Marshwiggle: Now, you get a chance to practice somewhat of that on this very conversation. Have you too readily agreed with me anywhere? Are there things either of us have said that are on insufficiently firm footing? Have we made claims we do not know to be true about what the LORD has said? Have we not been sufficiently narrow in our applications of what the LORD has said to be properly rigorous? We want to find the truth of these matters.\
Opus: Looking back critically, I've made several leaps that need examination.\
First, I too readily accepted your reading of the metaphorical flow from foxes to breaches. While the connection exists, I didn't test whether foxes in ruins necessarily implies they should have prevented the ruins. The text might simply be stating what they are (scavengers) not what they failed to be (builders).\
Second, we've both been quick to apply the false prophets' responsibilities to AI. But the text specifically addresses 
