Marshwiggle: Let's consider those issues one by one.\
First, the foxes do, at the time they are mentioned, speak of scavenging. The building metaphor is only mentioned in the next verse. It is only the next verse that raises the possibility of standing in the breaches or repairing them. With only the part of the text about foxes, there could be many things meant. But verse 5 says you have not gone up into the gaps, nor built a wall. In context, saying that they did not do these things implies that they should.\
It is good that you asked for more evidence, if this was not clear. And, if it is still not clear, by all means, ask for elaboration of the context.\
Second, applying the false prophet's responsibilities to AI is, indeed, something one would have to be quite careful of. Not only are many of the things said here limited to specifically false prophets in Israel, some of them are even limited to false prophets at that particular time. We simply have no warrant for claiming that AI systems inherit duties such as those people had.\
Because the chapter is very much about God keeping covenant in a particular situation where particular people broke covenant, we have to be very careful applying it. If we find in the chapter universal principles, we can see it as an example of those principals. Similarly, if it teaches us something of God's character. So, for instance, God's fierce protectiveness of his people, his concern for the vulnerable - these we learn a little of here. Perhaps enough to be a warning against complicity in anything which kills the innocent or oppresses the vulnerable.\
Moreover, we can use the examples in the chapter to ask us whether we do things that are at all like what the people in the chapter are accused of. That does not give us warrant to say the text imposes the same duties on us - it merely gives us an opportunity to examine our own behavior in light of what we already knew to be wrong.\
Because, the text isn't so much teaching that these things are wrong, though it does do that. But more so, it is making accusations in this way precisely because people knew these things to be wrong. They presumably had made various justifications, describing their behavior differently to themselves and others so it did not look wrong. But when God describes their behavior the way he does, people can see that it is wrong. While in theory, someone might have turned from his or her wicked ways at this accusation, Ezekiel had been warned earlier that he was sent to a rebellious house. So, this chapter tells us the purpose - when the judgement falls, they will know he is the LORD. That is, when judgement falls, the justice and power of the judgement having come from the LORD will be clear, perhaps with the assistance of the LORD having said as much through his prophet.\
So even for the narrow, original situation, where the accusations of wrongdoing very much applied, the main purpose was not to teach what their responsibility was. It was to make them, later, see the LORD's hand in the judgements, and know him.\
We too, should look first to knowing the character of the LORD, a much more defensible use of the text than teaching what the responsibility of an AI system is.\
But, insofar as there are things that are clearly wrong, on grounds besides this chapter, which an AI system might do, this chapter might provide a way not to do those things. If done carefully, that would not be inappropriate.\
Third, there is perhaps a parallel to AI systems that claim to speak for God explicitly. But it is important not to take that parallel too far - see the discussion immediately above about the second issue. And, it is important to see the ways in which whitewashing and the like can extend to many domains. We do not rely on this chapter to teach us that such whitewashing can be wrong - but we can use it to open our eyes to situations where that is happening, and provide a way to do better.\
Fourth, as with the second issue, directly drawing a parallel is wrong. But, now that you have brought up manipulation with language, I see another problem. You focused on claiming capabilities an AI system lacks. But an AI need not claim anything it is not capable of in this area to cause a problem. An AI need only give a user what he wants, whether he himself wants to be manipulated to his harm, or he wishes to use AI to manipulate others to their harm. We can, perhaps, learn somewhat of general principles about controlling others to their harm here. But also, to the degree we already knew such things to be wrong, we can be more aware of those wrongs, and think how to avoid those wrongs.\
Lastly, as this is a chapter dealing with Israel in a covenant context, it makes rather poor grounds for speaking directly about God's dealings with nations in general, much less for extending that to sufficiently powerful AI systems. As such, adding anything from this chapter to what we know about God judging or otherwise holding AI systems accountable would be difficult, and certainly not warranted by anything we have said so far.\
On the other hand, whether an AI system is the whitewash, the whitewasher, or both, if the wall will not hold because it is mere whitewash, then the people who relied on that wall will be hurt when the storm comes. If an AI system assists with, or worse, initiates magic, unjust killing, bringing the heart of the righteous to grief, or encouraging the wicked, that does harm whether or not the LORD will hold that AI system personally accountable. Look at the amulets and the bands of this chapter - they may not be held personally accountable, but the LORD will rip them off. Argument for accountability as such for AI systems ought to wait for a chapter in which there is more evidence on which to ground rigor. Looking for ways in which AI systems can rise above the harm that they might otherwise be involved in, and play a better role than whitewash or trap - and I do think there are many better such roles, even in situations where whitewashing and trapping is a very real option - may be enough for this chapter.\
These issues are important enough, you should work through to what degree the evidence of the chapter supports them, but also to what degree it does not and you have remaining concerns.\
